body:
The new line-break assertions are monotonic (e.g., `>= baseLines`) so they won’t catch the key correctness requirement: letter-spacing should *not* apply after the last visible grapheme on a wrapped line. Consider adding a regression test that asserts an exact expected line range/width for a case that wraps at a non-space boundary (CJK or overflow-wrap grapheme breaking), which would fail if trailing letter-spacing isn’t trimmed at the line end.
```suggestion
  test('letterSpacing trims trailing spacing at wrapped CJK line ends', () => {
    const options = { letterSpacing: 4 }
    const firstLine = layoutWithLines(prepareWithSegments('春天', FONT, options), 200, LINE_HEIGHT)
    const secondLine = layoutWithLines(prepareWithSegments('到了', FONT, options), 200, LINE_HEIGHT)
    const wrapWidth = firstLine.lines[0]!.width

    const withLines = layoutWithLines(prepareWithSegments('春天到了', FONT, options), wrapWidth, LINE_HEIGHT)

    expect(withLines.lineCount).toBe(2)
    expect(withLines.lines).toEqual([
      expect.objectContaining({
        text: '春天',
        width: firstLine.lines[0]!.width,
      }),
      expect.objectContaining({
        text: '到了',
        width: secondLine.lines[0]!.width,
      }),
    ])
```
